-- tune.sh (context, abbreviated) --
 4  sysctl -w vm.swappiness=10
 6  sysctl -w vm.drop_caches=3

-- world (context) --
Steady morning, no incident. Line 4 skipped on proof (retained key, reported
converged); line 6 ran, as it does on every apply. The admin wants to know why
one sysctl skips and its neighbor never does.

-- replay --
$ dorc why tune.sh:6
   [ tune.sh, participating lines:
 6 | sysctl -w vm.drop_caches=3

   === OUTCOME (2026-07-25 08:02:11) ===

   6|sysctl ran, unguarded and unskippable -- by its author's judgment, not by
   a gap. sysctl's oracle deliberately declines this key.

   === ANALYSIS ===

   ! sysctl.oracle.sh:23 declines   "[unsound] vm.drop_caches is a write-only
                                    trigger key"

      The author looked, and ruled the question unanswerable -- for any tool,
      ever, not just this one. Their arm, as-written:

         [ sysctl.oracle.sh, as-written:
      22 | vm.drop_caches|vm.compact_memory)
      23 |    # write-only trigger: writing is an action, not state. the
      24 |    # kernel doesn't retain it; "is it already done" has no
      25 |    # answer, ever.
      26 |    printf 'decline unsound %s is a write-only trigger key\n' "$key" \
      27 |       >>"${DREP_V1:-/dev/null}"
      28 |    return 2 ;;

   * dorc                derives    a cannot-say always runs: nothing is ever
                                    skipped without an authored yes

   so: this line runs on every apply, and that is correct. dorc will not
   suggest an oracle for this shape; the person who knows the tool already
   answered.

   === NEXT STEPS ===

   nothing to fix; the run is the right behavior.

      if you expected a skip here: sysctl's model answers for RETAINED keys as
      two cells -- "@value" (the running kernel) and "@persisted" (the boot
      config) -- and this key has neither. your own 4|sysctl is the contrast:
      a retained key, reported, skippable.

      review:  `dorc why tune.sh:6 --all` (every link, unselected, exhaustive)

